    "description": "Iran’s Constitutional Revolution of 1905–1911 sought to replace arbitrary royal government with representative institutions and government under law. It established a parliament, but faced internal divisions, royal resistance, and foreign intervention.",
    "tags": ["Constitutionalism", "Qajar Iran", "Parliament", "Political history", "Russian Empire"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T13:54:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T13:54:12Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Constitutional Revolution, 1905–1911 | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How protests against arbitrary government became a constitutional movement, established Iran’s parliament, and confronted royal and imperial power."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "constitutional-project",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making government answerable",
        "content": "The Constitutional Revolution changed the central question of Iranian politics: who could exercise public power, and under what rules? Beginning in 1905, a diverse movement challenged the arbitrary authority of the Qajar monarchy, the dynasty then governing Iran. Its defining achievement was the establishment of the Majles, Iran’s parliament, and a constitutional framework intended to place government under law. The conventional periodization, 1905–1911, encompasses protest, institution building, armed resistance, and severe reversals rather than one uninterrupted victory. [s1]\n\nConstitutionalists connected representative government with social justice and national independence. Foreign encroachment made these aims inseparable: reformers wanted institutions capable of resisting imperial pressure as well as restraining domestic rulers. Yet their coalition contained different understandings of religion, sovereignty, education, and political participation. Agreement that existing government required change did not produce agreement about the society that should replace it. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "protest-and-organization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From grievances to a political movement",
        "content": "The movement grew from practical grievances as well as political ideas. State financial difficulties, customs administration, and commercial disruption sharpened discontent. Merchants helped organize and finance opposition, while influential Shi‘i clerics supplied leadership and public meeting networks. Shi‘i clerics were religious scholars whose authority could extend beyond worship into law and community affairs. [s2]\n\nIn December 1905, the punishment of Tehran merchants accused of raising sugar prices helped trigger open resistance. Bazaar closures and gatherings made the dispute a public confrontation. Protesters also used bast, the practice of taking sanctuary in a protected place, to organize collectively and press demands. An early demand for a 'house of justice' remained sufficiently ambiguous to accommodate different expectations; a fully articulated parliamentary program did not exist at the outset. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-struggle",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A revolution beyond Tehran",
        "content": "The struggle cannot be understood through the capital alone. After the June 1908 attack on constitutional government, resistance developed in Iranian Azerbaijan and Gilan in the north and among Bakhtiyari forces associated with the southwest. Constitutionalists recaptured Tehran and deposed Mohammad Ali Shah in 1909. Volunteers arriving from the Caucasus also participated, connecting Iranian events with revolutionary networks across the Russian frontier. [s3]\n\nForeign intervention did not follow a single, unchanging script. Archival research shows disagreement and poor coordination among Russian officials before their increasingly coercive turn. By late 1911, Russian demands targeted Iran’s financial adviser Morgan Shuster and constrained the government’s freedom to appoint advisers. Military intervention in northern Iran and the suppression of constitutionalist resistance exposed the limits of sovereignty under imperial pressure. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "constitutional-sequence",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From protest to constitutional government and armed resistance",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"1905: Public protests expand\"] -->|\"Constitutional demands develop\"| B[\"1906: First Majles convenes\"]\n  B -->|\"Royal counterattack\"| C[\"June 1908: Constitutional government overthrown\"]\n  C -->|\"Armed constitutionalist resistance\"| D[\"1909: Tehran recaptured and shah deposed\"]\n  D -->|\"Renewed institution building under pressure\"| E[\"1911: Russian intervention and constitutional crisis\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show the order of major phases, with labels identifying the political transitions. The sequence summarizes a contested process rather than suggesting that each outcome was inevitable.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-legacy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An achievement with unresolved tensions",
        "content": "The revolution’s importance lies in establishing law and representation as explicit standards against which government could be judged. Its limitations also matter: the constitutional coalition was divided, and creating a parliament did not by itself secure effective administration or protection from coercion. The period is best read as an unfinished effort to reconcile accountable government, religious authority, and independence under difficult conditions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
